}
\details{
\code{Normalizer} stores fitted scaling parameters internally (\code{private$.state},
inspectable via \verb{$get_state()}) after \verb{$fit()} or \verb{$fit_transform()}:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Z-score / Robust scaling (\code{method = "z_score"}):} standardizes
each column using column mean and standard deviation (\code{robust = FALSE})
or column median and MAD (\code{robust = TRUE}), falling back to standard
deviation when MAD is non-finite/zero and setting zero/non-finite
dispersion to \code{1} with a warning.
\item \strong{Min-max scaling (\code{method = "min_max"}):} rescales each column
using observed column minimum and maximum (\code{lo} and \code{hi}), setting range
to \code{1} with a warning when a column has zero range.
}

\strong{Valid and missing values.} \code{NA}, \code{NaN} and \verb{+/-Inf} are treated as
missing: they are ignored when fitting and returned as \code{NA} in the output.
A column with no finite values yields \code{NA} for all its entries; an input
with no finite value at all raises an error. Warnings identify the
affected columns by name (or by index if the matrix has no column names).

\strong{Columns are matched by position, not by name.} The fitted parameters
are stored per column index. \verb{$transform()} and \verb{$inverse()} only check that
the number of columns equals the one seen at fit time; column names are not
compared. The input must therefore keep the same column order used in
\verb{$fit()}, otherwise the result is silently wrong.

\strong{Shape.} A plain vector is treated as a single column and returned as a
vector; a matrix (including a one-column matrix) is returned as a matrix.
}
